    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1 style="font-size: var(--step-h2);">Who Built This, and Why</h1>
    <p class="panel__lede">Webpage Anatomy is a free, interactive teaching tool, built by one person, for anyone trying to understand how a webpage actually goes together.</p>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="panel">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">The short version</p>
    <h2>Built by Creativepea</h2>
    <div class="panel__body">
      <p>I run Creativepea, a small web design studio, alongside its technical sister business, DevNinja. I'm also a graduate of The Brief Collective's DBA University, a coaching program for brand designers. Most of my work is client projects, but this page exists because I kept explaining the same concepts over and over, and a static explanation never landed the way a page you can actually take apart does.</p>
      <p>So this page teaches by letting you poke at it. Hover a marker and see what it is. Toggle an outline and watch semantic HTML stop being invisible. Answer a quiz question and get graded instantly. That is the whole idea: show, don't just tell.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">The practical version</p>
    <h2>How This Page Stays Free</h2>
    <div class="panel__body">
      <p>Webpage Anatomy does not run ads and does not sell anything on its own. A few links on the <a href="/resources/">Resources</a> page are affiliate links, marked with an asterisk, and there is a Ko-fi link if this page saved you time and you want to say thanks. Neither is required to use anything here.</p>
      <p>Everything on this page is fact-checked against real sources, not guessed at. Where a claim needed a source, one is linked. Where a demo could be built to actually work instead of just being described, it was built that way.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

